==English==
[[File:John Horwood’s funeral 2.jpg|thumb|300px|The obsequies observed for [[w:John Horwood|John Horwood]] on April 13, 2011]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|obsequie||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sekʷ-|id=follow}}
From {{uder|en|la|obsequiī||complaisant, yielding}}, alteration of {{m|la|obsequia||compliance}} (by confusion, in association with {{m|la|exequiae|exsequia|funeral rites}}, from {{m|la|exsequī||follow or accompany to the grave}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɔbsɪ.kwiː/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|obsequies}}

# The last [[office]] for the [[dead]].
# {{lb|en|chiefly|in the plural}} A [[funeral]] [[rite]] or [[service]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1478|author=[[w:Geoffrey Chaucer|Geoffrey Chaucer]]|title=[[w:The Canterbury Tales|The Canterbury Tales]]|passage=And to the ladyes he reſtored agayn / The bodyes of her huſbandes [[that|y&#877;]] were ſlayn / To done '''obſequies''' as tho was the [[gyse|gyſe]].|t=And to the ladies he restored again / The bodies of their husbands that were slain / To do '''obsequies''' as then was the custom.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=H. Rider Haggard|authorlink=H. Rider Haggard|title=She: A History of Adventure|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/3155/3155-0.txt|passage=It was a rather ghastly ceremony, but it was the only means in our power of showing our respect to the faithful dead and of celebrating his '''obsequies'''.}}
#* '''1919 ''', [[w:Ronald Firbank|Ronald Firbank]], ''[[w:Valmouth|Valmouth]]'', Duckworth, hardback edition, page 13:
#*: But, to-day, there were no '''obsequies''' to observe at all.

====Usage notes====
In modern usage, the word is used mainly in the plural&nbsp;– {{m|en|obsequies}}&nbsp;– which should not be confused with {{m|en|obsequious}}.

